  "text": "8.3 Welcome to Outer Space Today. Normally, this program discusses going into space. We talk about making long journeys into outer space, traveling well beyond our moon and the planets near us. However, in this episode, we're looking at the issue of space debris, or space junk. How did it first start? And what happens to all the things that people put into space? Humans first began to explore near space in 1957, when the Soviets sent the first human-made satellite, Sputnik 1, into space. Three months later, the satellite stopped working and fell back to Earth. It didn't hit the ground because it burned up on the way to Earth. This was the first rubbish, or junk, from space. But when Karabi Sputnik 1, or Sputnik 4, fell to Earth in 1962, a piece of it fell in the middle of a street in the central part of the U.S. Fortunately, it didn't hit anybody. Nowadays, there are many pieces of space junk from many countries going around the Earth and falling to Earth. This has many effects on our space programs and causes space accidents.",
